# Signed data has to survive a checkout byte for byte.
#
# A minisign signature covers the exact bytes of the file it was made over, so
# a checkout that rewrites line endings - which is what git does by default on
# Windows - produces a file its own signature no longer matches. These are the
# files where that matters, and `-text` tells git to leave them alone.
#
# This is not hypothetical: it failed on the Windows runner and nowhere else,
# and it failed in a way that looked like a bad signature rather than a bad
# checkout. The negative tests all still passed there, because a fixture that
# cannot verify is exactly what they expect to see.
tests/fixtures/signed-message.txt    -text
tests/fixtures/*.minisig             -text
assets/release-signing-key.pub       -text
